1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,840
“I didn’t like the ending,” I said to my favorite college professor.
“我不喜欢那个结局，”我对大学里最喜欢的那位教授说。

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:11,140
It was my junior year of college, and I was doing an independent study on Victorian literature.
那是我大学三年级的时候，我正在做一项关于维多利亚时代文学的独立研究。

3
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:17,190
I had just finished reading The Mill on the Floss by George Eliot, and I was heartbroken by the ending.
我刚读完乔治·艾略特的《弗洛斯河上的磨坊》，那个结局让我心碎。

4
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:23,900
Prof. Gracie, with all his patience, asked me to think about it beyond whether I liked it or not.
格雷西教授极有耐心地让我跳出“喜欢不喜欢”的层面，去思考这个结局。

5
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:37,200
He suggested I think about the difference between endings that I wanted for the characters and endings that were right for the characters, endings that satisfied the story even if they didn’t have a traditionally positive outcome.
他建议我思考两种结局之间的区别：一种是我希望人物得到的结局，另一种是对人物而言恰当的结局，也就是能满足故事需要的结局，哪怕这样的结局在传统意义上并不算好结果。

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,310
Of course, I would have preferred a different ending for Tom and Maggie Tulliver, but the ending they got did make the most sense for them.
当然，我本来更希望汤姆和玛吉·塔利弗有一个不同的结局，但他们得到的那个结局对他们来说确实是最合理的。

7
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:50,630
This was an aha moment for me, and I never thought about endings the same way again.
那一刻我恍然大悟，从此我看待结局的方式再也和从前不一样了。

8
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:57,180
From then on, if I wanted to read an ending guaranteed to be happy, I’d pick up a romance.
从那以后，如果我想读一个保证圆满的结局，我就会拿起一本爱情小说。

9
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:01,540
If I wanted an ending I couldn’t guess, I’d pick up a mystery.
如果想要一个猜不到的结局，我就会拿起一本悬疑小说。

10
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:06,340
One where I kind of knew what was going to happen, historical fiction.
如果想要一个我大致知道会发生什么的结局，就选历史小说。

11
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:09,110
Choosing what to read became easier.
选择读什么书变得更容易了。

12
00:01:09,910 --> 00:01:12,150
But writing the end — that’s hard.
但写结局——那可就难了。

13
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:16,800
It’s hard for writers because endings carry so much weight with readers.
这对作家来说很难，因为结局在读者心中分量很重。

14
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:25,300
You have to balance creating an ending that’s unpredictable, but doesn’t seem to come from nowhere, one that fits what’s right for the characters.
你必须把握好平衡，创作出这样一个结局：它出人意料，却又不显得凭空而来，而且符合人物应有的走向。

15
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:33,500
That’s why this issue of Writer’s Digest aims to help you figure out how to write the best ending for whatever kind of writing you’re doing.
正因如此，本期《作家文摘》旨在帮助你弄清楚：无论你在写哪种类型的作品，都该如何写出最好的结局。

16
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:41,050
If it’s short stories, Peter Mountford breaks down six techniques you can try to see which one helps you stick the landing.
如果你写的是短篇小说，彼得·蒙特福德剖析的六种技巧可供你一试，看看哪一种能帮你稳稳“落地”、漂亮收尾。

17
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:50,080
Elizabeth Sims analyzes the final chapters of five great novels to see what key points they include and how you can adapt them for your work.
伊丽莎白·西姆斯分析了五部伟大小说的最后几章，看看其中包含了哪些要点，以及你可以如何把这些要点加以调整，运用到自己的作品中。

18
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:57,950
This issue won’t tell you what your ending should be — that’s up to you and the story you’re telling — but it might provide what you need to get there.
本期不会告诉你结局应该是什么样的——那取决于你和你正在讲述的故事——但它可能会为你提供写出这个结局所需的帮助。
